DSL Compiler Simulation – Conditional Expression 

1. Problem Understanding 
This project simulates a simple Domain Specific Language (DSL) compiler that evaluates a conditional 
expression using the ternary operator. 

The DSL program: 
  max = (a > b) ? a : b 

is used to determine the maximum of two numbers. The goal is to demonstrate how a compiler processes 
this statement through different phases such as lexical analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, and code 
generation. 

2. Input Program 
a = 8 
b = 5 
max = a > b ? a : b 

3. Compiler Phases 

Lexical Analysis 
• Identifies tokens: 
o Identifiers → a, b, max 
o Numbers → 8, 5 
o Operators → > 
o Conditional symbols → ?, : 

Syntax Parsing 
• Recognizes the structure: 
max = (a > b) ? a : b 

Semantic Analysis 
• Checks: 
o Variables are valid ✔ 
o Data types are integers ✔ 
o Condition is logically correct ✔ 

Intermediate Code Generation 
if a > b goto L1 
t1 = b 
goto L2 
L1: t1 = a 
L2: max = t1 

4. Final Output 
max = 8 

5. Logic Used 
• If a > b, assign a to max 
• Otherwise, assign b to max 
• Implemented using an if-else condition in C 


6. Justification of Feature 
The ternary conditional operator is chosen because: 
• It represents real compiler decision-making 
• Demonstrates branching logic 
• Easy to simulate in compiler phases 
• Commonly used in programming languages 

Conclusion 
This project demonstrates how a compiler evaluates conditional expressions step-by-step. It provides a 
clear understanding of tokenization, parsing, semantic checking, and execution in a simplified manner.

